== Interactive Segmentation: From Graph Cuts to Transformers

The interactive segmentation paradigm began with energy-minimization formulations on graphs. Boykov and Jolly @boykov2001graphcuts cast foreground extraction as a min-cut/max-flow problem over a pixel-adjacency graph, where user-supplied seed clicks define hard constraints on source and sink terminals. Rother et al. @rother2004grabcut extended this framework with GrabCut, replacing fixed seeds with iterative Gaussian mixture model estimation inside a user-drawn bounding box and permitting corrective clicks to refine the result. These methods established the core interaction loop, click then refine, that persists in modern systems, yet they relied on hand-crafted color and texture likelihoods that generalized poorly to complex scenes.

The transition to learned representations began with Xu et al. @xu2016dios, who proposed Deep Interactive Object Selection (DIOS), encoding positive and negative clicks as Euclidean distance-transform maps concatenated with the RGB input to a CNN. This click-encoding strategy became the de facto standard adopted by subsequent methods. Jang and Kim @jang2019brs introduced backpropagating refinement (BRS), performing test-time gradient updates on an auxiliary click-correction layer so that the network output remains consistent with every provided click. Sofiiuk et al. @sofiiuk2020fbrs accelerated this idea with feature-level backpropagating refinement (f-BRS), restricting optimization to a small set of intermediate feature scales and achieving real-time interaction. More recently, Huang et al. @huang2023interformer proposed InterFormer, which decouples heavyweight image feature extraction from lightweight click-conditioned decoding, enabling the refinement stage to run entirely on a CPU while the backbone executes once on a GPU. Together, these advances established the principle that _prompt processing_ and _image understanding_ benefit from architectural separation, a design philosophy that SAM @kirillov2023sam later adopted at scale with its separate image encoder and prompt-conditioned mask decoder.

Despite its architectural maturity, SAM inherits the longstanding sensitivity to prompt placement that has accompanied click-based methods since graph cuts. Fan et al. @fan2025stablesam provide a rigorous analysis of this fragility, demonstrating that low-quality point prompts bias the decoder's cross-attention toward background tokens and proposing decoder-side plugins (DSP and DRP) to stabilize predictions. Complementing this model-centric view, Antonov et al. @antonov2024rclicks release a dataset of 475K real annotator clicks and show that model accuracy drops substantially when evaluated on realistic click distributions rather than the synthetic center-of-error-region clicks used during training. These findings confirm that prompt sensitivity is not merely a theoretical concern but a measurable deployment bottleneck. As discussed above, methods such as RITM @sofiiuk2022ritm, FocalClick @chen2022focalclick, and SimpleClick @liu2023simpleclick address this problem by retraining the segmentation backbone end-to-end. Our work takes a complementary path, treating SAM as a frozen model and learning a prompt-placement policy that mitigates sensitivity without modifying any model weights.
